package rowrules

// This file is yours.
//
// It holds the condition each resource puts on its own rows. For "may this
// kind of person use this endpoint", see internal/authz; for "is this
// particular record an exception", see internal/policies. Only the rules here
// can filter a list, because only these are applied to the query.
//
// Each resource gets five rules, one per action. An empty rule adds no
// condition: the resource's ordinary authorisation applies and nothing
// further happens, which is what every resource starts with. Filling one in
// is how you say "and only these rows".
//
//	Register("Order", Rules{
//	    List:   `@request.auth.id != "" && user_id = @request.auth.id`,
//	    View:   `user_id = @request.auth.id`,
//	    Create: `@request.auth.id != ""`,
//	    Update: `user_id = @request.auth.id && status = "draft"`,
//	    Delete: `user_id = @request.auth.id && status = "draft"`,
//	})
//
// The language is the one ?filter= uses, documented at /docs/backend/filtering.
// Briefly:
//
//	user_id = @request.auth.id        the row belongs to the caller
//	author.country = "UG"             a condition on a related row
//	members.user_id ?= @request.auth.id   any one of the related rows
//	status != "archived" && total > 0
//	created_at >= @monthStart
//	@request.body.role:changed = false    may be submitted, may not be altered
//
// Three things worth knowing before you write one:
//
//  1. A rule is applied to the query, not checked afterwards. For a list it
//     filters; for one record it means the row is not found, which is a 404
//     rather than a 403. A 403 on a row you may not read confirms the row
//     exists, and for an invoice number that is the whole of the information.
//
//  2. An ADMIN, and the application acting on its own behalf in a job or a
//     command, are not subject to these. A nightly invoice run reads every
//     invoice, and "only your own" would make it send none.
//
//  3. A create rule is evaluated before the row exists, so it can only talk
//     about the request: @request.auth and @request.body. Naming a column
//     there is reported rather than quietly ignored.
//
// Every rule is parsed at start-up. A rule that does not parse is a line in
// the log before the first request, and the query it guards is refused rather
// than run unfiltered.
